WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.753
Module 1: framing the study.

00:00:01.753 --> 00:00:04.819
The stories can only answer
the question you ask.

00:00:04.819 --> 00:00:09.764
In about twenty-five minutes you'll learn
when Mosaic Signal is the right tool,

00:00:09.764 --> 00:00:12.643
how to turn a client's
question into a prompt,

00:00:12.643 --> 00:00:15.647
and how to check a template
before it goes live.

00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:21.635
Mosaic Signal is strongest when the client
needs to know what is happening and what

00:00:21.635 --> 00:00:24.817
it means to people, across
many people, quickly.

00:00:24.817 --> 00:00:28.860
If they ask how strong their
culture is and how they compare,

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.451
that's a Culture Mosaic.

00:00:30.451 --> 00:00:35.820
If they ask whether a change moved the
scores, that's a Culture Progress Monitor.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:41.322
If they ask what their culture is really
doing, layer by layer, that's Culture Dig.

00:00:41.322 --> 00:00:45.763
And if it's one team's conflict,
that's a conversation, not a link.

00:00:45.763 --> 00:00:50.404
The best sign Mosaic Signal fits is
a client who already has a number,

00:00:50.404 --> 00:00:53.983
like a low tile or rising
turnover, and wants the why.

00:00:53.983 --> 00:00:56.701
They can reach at least dozens of people,

00:00:56.701 --> 00:01:00.280
and they're open to hearing
things they didn't expect.

00:01:00.767 --> 00:01:03.362
Three requests land in one week.

00:01:03.362 --> 00:01:06.200
For each, choose the best response.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:11.683
Before anyone touches a template,
answer five questions with your client.

00:01:11.683 --> 00:01:14.413
What decision will these stories inform?

00:01:14.413 --> 00:01:16.119
Whose stories do we need?

00:01:16.119 --> 00:01:18.099
What moment holds the answer?

00:01:18.099 --> 00:01:20.966
What do we want to see across the stories?

00:01:20.966 --> 00:01:25.880
And what will we do with what we find,
including the unflattering parts?

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.064
For Northwind's decisions study,

00:01:28.064 --> 00:01:31.887
the moment is a time a decision
affected someone's work.

00:01:31.887 --> 00:01:33.525
That becomes the prompt.

00:01:33.525 --> 00:01:36.596
What they want to see,
what shaped decisions,

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:42.057
when people heard and what they did next,
becomes the questions about the story.

00:01:42.057 --> 00:01:44.924
The last question is the one clients skip.

00:01:44.924 --> 00:01:45.879
Ask it anyway.

00:01:45.879 --> 00:01:51.476
A study nobody is ready to act on teaches
staff that telling their stories changes

00:01:51.476 --> 00:01:52.022
nothing.

00:01:52.533 --> 00:01:54.510
Here's something easy to miss.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:59.518
A respondent reads three pieces of text
for one story question: the question

00:01:59.518 --> 00:02:01.956
heading, a grey description under it,

00:02:01.956 --> 00:02:05.910
and the prompt in a highlighted
box above the writing space.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:10.852
They all come from the template, and
editing one doesn't change the others.

00:02:10.852 --> 00:02:14.543
On their first survey, Northwind
edited only the prompt,

00:02:14.543 --> 00:02:17.837
so the heading asked
something slightly different.

00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:19.814
Read all three aloud together.

00:02:19.814 --> 00:02:23.175
They should sound like one
person asking one thing.

00:02:23.667 --> 00:02:26.190
You know the basics of a good prompt.

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:30.760
Here are five habits from the Mosaic
guidance that take it further.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:35.739
Use the organization's own words, like
the floor, the unit or the clinic,

00:02:35.739 --> 00:02:40.036
because borrowed corporate language
gets outside-voice answers.

00:02:40.036 --> 00:02:44.128
Make it safe to be unflattering:
saying a story can be good,

00:02:44.128 --> 00:02:48.221
bad or mixed roughly doubles
the range of what people share.

00:02:48.221 --> 00:02:49.721
One prompt, one story.

00:02:49.721 --> 00:02:51.836
Don't name the answer you want,

00:02:51.836 --> 00:02:55.928
or you'll find exactly what you
asked for and learn nothing.

00:02:55.928 --> 00:02:59.816
And never promise anonymity
your distribution can't keep.

00:02:59.816 --> 00:03:02.067
Two or three sentences is plenty.

00:03:02.567 --> 00:03:06.535
You can offer more than one
prompt and let people choose.

00:03:06.535 --> 00:03:09.529
The chosen prompt shows
on each story card,

00:03:09.529 --> 00:03:14.681
but the charts pool every prompt
together, and you can't filter by prompt.

00:03:14.681 --> 00:03:18.928
So use multiple prompts only
for close variations of one ask.

00:03:18.928 --> 00:03:23.871
Two very different prompts give you
charts that mix two kinds of story.

00:03:23.871 --> 00:03:25.959
Word limits are guidance only.

00:03:25.959 --> 00:03:31.320
The counter turns amber or red, but
SurveyCraft accepts the story either way.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.757
Set them, and keep the minimum low.

00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:38.716
Templates are tested shapes, but
read every word before you launch.

00:03:38.716 --> 00:03:44.029
Check the wording: the Organizational
Culture Probe's description asks people to

00:03:44.029 --> 00:03:47.881
say who was involved, which
you'll usually want to remove.

00:03:47.881 --> 00:03:52.396
Check the context questions: the
Change Readiness template has none,

00:03:52.396 --> 00:03:57.045
so if you want to compare clinics
with the hospital, add the question,

00:03:57.045 --> 00:03:58.174
as Northwind did.

00:03:58.174 --> 00:04:00.830
Check that the questions fit your topic.

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:05.878
And check the order: story first, then
the questions about it, then context,

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:08.069
with Shuffle Blocks switched off.

00:04:08.567 --> 00:04:12.464
Jamie has drafted the decisions
study's story question,

00:04:12.464 --> 00:04:18.275
and the prompt asks for a time a decision
was made without asking frontline staff.

00:04:18.275 --> 00:04:23.731
Rewrite all three pieces of text so they
ask one thing, in Northwind's words,

00:04:23.731 --> 00:04:25.574
without naming the answer.

00:04:25.574 --> 00:04:28.054
Then compare with the model answer.

00:04:28.567 --> 00:04:33.425
Time for the module check: five
questions, and you need four right.

00:04:33.933 --> 00:04:34.594
To recap.

00:04:34.594 --> 00:04:40.468
Mosaic Signal answers why, and what it's
like, not how much or whether it moved.

00:04:40.468 --> 00:04:42.890
Fill in the framing canvas first.

00:04:42.890 --> 00:04:46.488
Make the heading, description
and prompt one ask.

00:04:46.488 --> 00:04:51.334
One prompt, one story, and remember
word limits are only guidance.

00:04:51.334 --> 00:04:54.197
And audit every template before launch.

00:04:54.197 --> 00:04:57.574
Next, designing the
questions about the story.
